Below is a step‐by‐step reasoning and the resulting goal model decomposition based on the mission description. In our design, we break the mission into two main execution branches: one for a single resource (sequential collection then delivery) and one for multiple resources (parallel collection‐delivery tasks). Each branch is modeled as a set of goals with associated tasks. We then explain the relationships and justifications.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Analyzing the Mission Description

• The mission requires a robot (or multiple robots) to:
 – Collect resources from one or more storages.
 – Deliver the resources to a specified destination.
 – Monitor battery levels during collection (threshold 10%) and delivery (threshold 30%).
 – Fallback if battery is low: in collection, return to a recharging station and reassign the mission; in delivery, return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remainder.
 – For multiple items from different storages, assign multiple robots to execute collection‐delivery tasks in parallel.

• Based on these points, we identify two execution strategies:
 – Sequential (for a single resource) where the collection phase must be completed before starting the delivery phase.
 – Parallel (for multiple resources) where independent robots perform collection and delivery concurrently.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Defining the Goals

We define four main goals:

1. G1: Mission Execution  
 – Overall mission goal to have all resources collected and delivered.
 – Since the mission can be executed either sequentially (for a single resource) or in parallel (for multiple resources), we use an OR decomposition at the top level.  
  
2. G2: Collection Phase  
 – Responsible for gathering resources from storage.
 – Activities include navigating to storage, requesting the resource, waiting for retrieval, and monitoring battery.
 – These steps occur sequentially (AND decomposition) because each must be completed before moving on.

3. G3: Delivery Phase  
 – Responsible for delivering collected resources.
 – Activities include navigating to the delivery location, delivering the resource, monitoring battery, and executing fallback if needed.
 – These steps are also sequential (AND decomposition).

4. G5: Parallel Execution for Multiple Items  
 – Activated when multiple items are required.
 – Each resource’s collection and delivery are handled concurrently by different robots.
 – The execution is parallel (using the “#” operator) and the tasks for each item are executed concurrently.

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Defining the Tasks

For the sequential branch (single resource), we define the following tasks under G2 and G3:

• Under G2 (Collection Phase):
 – AT1: Navigate to Storage  
 – AT2: Request Resource  
 – AT3: Wait for Retrieval  
 – AT4: Monitor Battery – Collection (with fallback if battery falls below 10%)  
 – AT9: Fallback Collection (if battery is low)

• Under G3 (Delivery Phase):
 – AT5: Navigate to Delivery Location  
 – AT6: Deliver Resource  
 – AT7: Monitor Battery – Delivery (with fallback if battery falls below 30%)  
 – AT8: Return to Checkpoint (fallback during delivery)  
 – AT10: Fallback Delivery (if battery is low)  
 – AT11: Alert Sector Manager (if fallback fails)

For the parallel branch (multiple items), we define parallelized versions of the tasks under G5:

• Parallel tasks in G5:
 – AT12: Parallel Navigate to Storage  
 – AT13: Parallel Request Resource  
 – AT14: Parallel Wait for Retrieval  
 – AT15: Parallel Monitor Battery  
 – AT16: Parallel Fallback Actions  
 – AT17: Parallel Delivery  
 – AT18: Parallel Alert Sector Manager (if fallback fails)

